---------------- Role Based Access Control ---------------------

. You can understand this the kuberenetes resources i.e. pods, deployments, services, statefulset etc. Do u have access to it or not so for that we need to define the particular rols for the users exist in the cluster.

----- To learn about the RBAC, u need to learn two things ---------------
1. Service Account => may be on our node, namespace(deployments) level.
    Roles => Role Binding

2. User => this will be on our cluster level
    Cluster Role => Cluster Role Binding
-------------------------------------------------------------------------


-------------------------- Major RBAC Commands --------------------------

1. kubectl auth whoami => This will tell you about the current user
- kubectl auth whoami
ATTRIBUTE                                           VALUE
Username                                            kubernetes-admin
Groups                                              [kubeadm:cluster-admins system:authenticated]
Extra: authentication.kubernetes.io/credential-id   [X509SHA256=57eac3afe7f9eb0c8e5c2313e8a5195e108d09c02b0fb243a761dac2844eec09]

2. kubectl auth can-i get pods
- kubectl auth can-i get pods
yes

3. kubectl auth can-i get pods -n nginx-dev 
- kubectl auth can-i get pods -n nginx-dev
yes

// This is too risky that suppose even any user have delete access role so they delete the deployments also so for this we have RBAC (need to create the role).
4. kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n nginx-dev  
- kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n nginx-dev
yes

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

----------------- To implemeent the Service Account we will work with previous environment that is nginx (namespace, deployments, services) ----------------------

---> vim role.yml
--->
kind: Role
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1  # ==> group
metadata:
    name: nginx-manager
    namespace: nginx-dev

rules:
    # "*" = means we are giving the access to all groups
    # "" = blank group means where the only v1 is written
    # "apps" = group that are started with apps/v1 something kind of
    # "batch" = for cronjob etc 
    - apiGroups: ["", "apps", "rbac.authorization.k8s.io", "batch"]

    # Now define access that u want to give
      resources: ["deployment", "pod", "service"]
      verbs: ["get", "apply", "delete", "watch", "create", "patch"]

---

So now it means any user who has this particular role (nginx-manager) can access the deployment, pod and service in order to apply the actions defined in verbs.

- Then run kubectl apply -f role.yml
- kubectl get role -n nginx-dev
NAME            CREATED AT
nginx-manager   2026-05-17T18:31:38Z

- We are creating the service account at the namespace level.
---> vim service-account.yml
--->
kind: ServiceAccount
apiVersion: v1
metadata:
    name: nginx-user
    namespace: nginx-dev

---

- kubectl apply -f service-account.yml
serviceaccount/nginx-user created
- root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster$ kubectl get serviceaccount -n nginx-dev
NAME         AGE
default      27m
nginx-user   17s

- Now check that nginx-user(service account) can get the pods or not
- kubectl auth can-i get pod -n nginx-dev --as=nginx-user
no

- So, we need to bind this particular role with user by role-binding
---> vim role-binding.yml
--->
kind: RoleBinding
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1

metadata:
  name: nginx-manager-rolebinding
  namespace: nginx-dev

subjects:
  - kind: User # service account
    name: nginx-user
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

# what role would u like to assign this user 
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: nginx-manager
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

---

- Then run kubectl apply -f role-binding.yml
- kubectl get rolebinding -n nginx-dev

- kubectl auth can-i get pods -n nginx-dev --as=nginx-user
yes
- kubectl auth can-i get deployment -n nginx-dev --as=nginx-user
yes

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

---------- If we need to do the monitoring, observability, metrics and logging so we need to understand the Cluster Role and Cluster Role Binding ---------------



